entries.body:
Unpacks array elements or object properties into variables in one statement.

const [a, b] = arr; const { name, age = 0 } = obj;
Defaults apply when the value is undefined. Rename: const { name: n } = obj.

Throw and catch errors with throw new Error(message) and try/catch.

try { throw new Error('nope'); } catch (e) { console.error(e.message); } finally { ... }
Custom errors: class MyError extends Error {}

Returns [key, value] pairs for an object's own enumerable properties.

Object.entries(obj) -> array

Common pattern: for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) { ... }

Expands an iterable or object into elements/properties: [...arr], {...obj}, fn(...args).

const copy = [...arr]; const merged = { ...a, ...b };
Rest parameters collect the opposite way: function f(...args) {}.

A function that returns a Promise; await pauses it until a Promise settles.

async function f() { const v = await g(); return v; }

await only works inside async functions (and top-level module code). A rejected await throws, so try/catch handles async errors.

Declares a class with a constructor and methods; extends sets up inheritance.

class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name) { super(); this.name = name; }
  speak() { return this.name + ' barks'; }
}
Methods live on the prototype; static methods live on the class itself.

Browser/Node HTTP client returning a Promise of a Response.

const res = await fetch(url, { method, headers, body });
const data = await res.json();

fetch does NOT reject on HTTP error statuses; check res.ok. It rejects only on network failure.

